WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
In an emission nebula, energetic ultraviolet light from hot

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:09.120
stars ionizes gas. Recombination and subsequent transitions produce characteristic

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.140
emission. The glowing gas supplies the visible light. A

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.980
reflection nebula redirects light from nearby stars toward us.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.460
Dust scattering makes these regions often appear blue. The

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.580
cloud is reflecting starlight rather than generating the same

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.500
gas emission spectrum. A dark nebula blocks background light

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.340
along our line of sight. The same dusty cloud

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.340
can look different with illumination and viewing geometry. Infrared

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.940
suffers less dust extinction than visible light, allowing observations

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.200
deeper inside; it does not pass through every cloud

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.728
completely unhindered.
